[38;5;10minputs: p1=(-1.000082e-20, -5.1826152e36) p2=(3.528892e28, -2.256278e19)
        p3=(NaN, -1.9677248e-22)          p=(-2.7035357e38, -1.8820893e-28)
C    = bits (0xffe757c9, 0xffe757c9)
Rust = bits (0xffe757c9, 0xffc00000)   <-- y lost the payload
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The cause is that on x86 SSE, when [3mboth[23m operands of a scalar FP instruction are NaN, the result is the quieted [1mdestination[22m register — so [38;5;10ma * b[0m and [38;5;10mb * a[0m are observably different. The C library (built at [38;5;10m-O0[0m, per [38;5;10mCMakeLists.txt[0m, which sets no optimization flags) has a fixed operand assignment, while Rust's optimizer commutes multiplications freely. I confirmed this from the C disassembly, which also revealed that [38;5;10mlm_dot2[0m is not uniformly "left operand is destination":[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10ma.x * b.x[0m → dest is [38;5;10ma.x[0m (left)[0m[0m
- [38;5;10ma.y * b.y[0m → dest is [38;5;10mb.y[0m (**right**)[0m[0m
- the final [38;5;10m+[0m → dest is the [38;5;10my[0m product (**right**)[0m[0m
- everything in [38;5;10mto_barycentric[0m and [38;5;10mlm_sub2[0m → dest is the left operand[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The fix routes every FP operation in [38;5;10msrc/lib.rs[0m through explicit [38;5;10msse_mul[0m/[38;5;10msse_add[0m/[38;5;10msse_sub[0m/[38;5;10msse_div[0m helpers that encode the hardware rule (dest NaN wins, then src NaN, otherwise the native op). This makes NaN behavior independent of Rust's codegen, and is bit-identical to the previous code for all non-NaN inputs.[0m[0m
